```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 22;
    int *p = &a;

    printf("%p\n", p); // Initial address of p
    p++;
    printf("%p\n", p); // Address after increment
    p--;
    printf("%p\n", p); // Address after decrement

    float b = 22.22;
    float *q = &b;

    printf("%p\n", q); // Initial address of q
    q++;
    printf("%p\n", q); // Address after increment
    q--;
    printf("%p\n", q); // Address after decrement

    char c = 'a';
    char *r = &c;

    printf("%p\n", r); // Initial address of r
    r++;
    printf("%p\n", r); // Address after increment
    r--;
    printf("%p\n", r); // Address after decrement

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation:
- They are demonstrating how pointer arithmetic works for different data types.
- For each type:
  - `int`: usually 4 bytes
  - `float`: usually 4 bytes
  - `char`: 1 byte
- When you do `p++`, you’re not just adding 1 — you’re adding `sizeof(type)` bytes.

